        "id": "fd_0346",
        "title": "Prime-Adelic Stability Law for Neural Network Decision Regions",
        "description": "Conjecture: Let F be a ReLU network with rational weights and biases defining a classification map on Q^n. There exists a finite exceptional set of primes S(F) such that for every prime p not in S(F), the reduction of F modulo p induces a polyhedral decision complex whose Betti numbers and adjacency graph are identical to those of the original rational decision complex after base change to Q_p. Test: Compute the exact decision-region cell complex over Q for many rational ReLU networks, reduce the same network modulo varying primes p, and compare combinatorial type, Betti numbers, and region adjacency. The conjecture is confirmed if all but finitely many primes preserve these invariants, and refuted by an infinite family of good-coefficient networks with infinitely many bad primes causing genuine topology changes. Impact: This would create an unexpected bridge between tropical/neural geometry and arithmetic stability, enabling prime-wise certification of network robustness, new arithmetic invariants of learned models, and a route to formal local-global principles for piecewise-linear learning systems.",
