        "description": "Conjecture: For any finitely presented terminating rewrite system R and any infinite family of terms {t_n} with |t_n| -> infinity, build the reduction DAG D_n of all reducts of t_n modulo sharing, and equip D_n with the path-length filtration from t_n. There exists a universal homological precursor of compression: whenever the first persistent Betti number of D_n exhibits a bar of length at least c log |D_n| (for some system-dependent constant c > 0), the minimal normal-form derivation of t_n admits a proof-compression ratio at least |t_n|^epsilon over tree-style normalization, for some epsilon > 0 depending only on R. Test: Compute reduction DAGs for benchmark convergent systems (lambda fragments, Knuth-Bendix completed equational theories, e-graph saturation traces), measure persistent H1 under the natural filtration, and check whether long H1 bars statistically predict superlinear compression in shared normalization; a single robust counterfamily with long bars but no compression, or compression without the predicted homology, refutes the claim. Impact: This would connect topological data analysis to proof theory and rewriting, giving a new invariant that forecasts when quotienting/sharing should dramatically accelerate symbolic computation.",
