        "id": "fd_0399",
        "title": "Chromatic Torsion Predicts Resolution Space in Hypergraph Coloring Encodings",
        "description": "Conjecture: For the standard CNF encoding Col_k(H) of non-k-colorability of a finite r-uniform hypergraph H, there exists a computable invariant tau(H,k) extracted from the p-primary torsion profile of the independence complex and coloring complex of H such that minimum clause space of any resolution refutation of Col_k(H) is bounded below by c\u00b7tau(H,k) for a universal constant c>0 on an infinite family of hypergraphs. Test: Compute torsion barcodes/primary decomposition for explicit families (including Pythagorean triple hypergraphs, Steiner systems, and random sparse uniform hypergraphs), generate the corresponding CNF encodings, and compare tau(H,k) against empirically or formally established resolution space lower bounds; the conjecture is refuted by a family with large torsion invariant but uniformly small clause space, or vice versa. Impact: This would create a new bridge from topological/combinatorial torsion invariants to proof complexity, giving a geometric predictor of SAT hardness and potentially new lower-bound techniques for coloring and Ramsey-type encodings.",
