        "id": "fd_0582",
        "title": "Arithmetic Universality in Torsion Persistence via Prime-Indexed Automata",
        "description": "Conjecture: There exists a finite filtered CW complex template T with integer boundary matrices of bounded entry size, together with a computable rule that substitutes a parameter n into T, such that for every ultimately periodic binary sequence a_p indexed by primes p, the p-primary torsion barcode of H_k(T(n); Z) realizes a_p on a prescribed finite window of primes up to poly(log n). Equivalently, primewise torsion persistence can encode arbitrary finite automaton behavior across the prime axis with only polynomial growth in complex size. Test: Construct explicit template families T(n), compute primewise persistent homology for many n and primes, and check whether the observed birth/death pattern matches the target automaton language; refute by proving any uniform logical/number-theoretic restriction forbids such prime-indexed pattern complexity. Impact: This would reveal torsion persistence as an arithmetic information processor rather than a passive invariant, linking topological data analysis, automata theory, and arithmetic geometry, and would give a new expressive-complexity lens for integer persistent homology.",
